Community blocklists from the Selfhostr network
===============================================

Base address: https://@FEEDS_DOMAIN@/v1/

These are free, public lists of internet addresses and host names that several independent
honeypot sensors saw attacking or serving malware. Anyone can download them. There is no account,
no key and no tracking. The same files are served to everyone.

THE LISTS
---------
inbound           Addresses seen attacking sensors (scans, brute force, exploit attempts).
inbound-high      A smaller, stricter set of the same, seen by more sensors for longer.
outbound          Addresses that served or controlled malware seen by sensors.
outbound-high     A smaller, stricter set of the same.
outbound-domains  Exact host names (no wildcards) that served or controlled malware.

An entry stays on a list for a limited time after it was last seen (days to a few weeks) and then
expires by itself. Shared infrastructure (public resolvers, CDNs, Tor exit nodes) is kept off.

FILES
-----
index.json            The manifest: serial, validity, and the SHA-256 and size of every file.
<list>.txt            One value per line. <list>.v4.txt and <list>.v6.txt split by family.
<list>.nft            nftables set, load with: nft -f
<list>.ipset          ipset restore file with an atomic swap
<list>.rsc            MikroTik RouterOS v7 script
<list>.csv / .json    Same values with details (first seen, last seen, expiry, categories)
outbound.stix.json    STIX 2.1 bundle (TLP:CLEAR)
stats.json            Public statistics, including the delisting figures
removed.json          Values that expired or were taken down in the last 30 days
delist.html           The delisting form
Every file has a .minisig signature, and a .gz copy for clients that ask for compression.

